  "preamble": "The transformation of a palatal glide [j] into a voiced palatal stop [ɟ] is a fortition (consonantalization), usually of glides at the beginning of a syllable or word. It is attested in Samoyedic (Proto-Samoyedic *j- in Nganasan and Forest Nenets) and in Albanian, where word-initial *j (from PIE *y and from *s) hardened to gj [ɟ] (*jama > gjem 'bridle').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fortition, Consonantalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Samoyedic to Nganasan / Forest Nenets",
      "languageFamily": "Samoyedic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*jalä 'sun'",
          "to": "Nganasan дялы; Forest Nenets дяԓя",
          "note": "Initial *j > dʲ; cf. Tundra Nenets яля"
        },
        {
          "from": "*jam 'sea'",
          "to": "Nganasan дям; Forest Nenets дя̄м",
          "note": "cf. Tundra Nenets ямʼ"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Albanian to Albanian",
      "languageFamily": "Albanian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *jama",
          "to": "gjem 'bridle'",
          "note": "From PIE *yem-."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *jausna",
          "to": "gjanë 'silt, alluvium'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Albanian *jāgnja",
          "to": "gjoj 'to hear, listen to'",
          "note": "Gheg gjue."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
